    level: "B2",
    text: "Cold Blood, Sharp Senses — Inside the Reptile House\n\nThe reptile house is one of the most fascinating — and misunderstood — sections of any zoo or wildlife centre. Behind glass-fronted terrariums fitted with heat lamps, misting systems, and carefully arranged branches, rocks, and moss, visitors encounter creatures whose evolutionary lineage stretches back over 300 million years. Reptiles may lack the fur and warmth that make mammals appealing, but their adaptations are no less remarkable.\n\nReptiles are ectothermic — they regulate body temperature using external heat sources rather than generating their own. This is why you will see lizards bask on sun-warmed rocks or under heat lamps: they absorb energy to power their metabolism. When they overheat, they retreat to a shaded hide or burrow into sand. This behaviour is not laziness — it is precise thermal regulation that allows reptiles to survive on a fraction of the food a mammal of equivalent size would require.\n\nSnakes exhibit extraordinary diversity. The king cobra — the world's longest venomous snake — delivers venom through hollow fangs, paralysing prey with neurotoxins. A python, by contrast, is a constrictor: it coils around its prey and squeezes until the animal can no longer breathe. The rattlesnake warns predators with a distinctive hiss and the rattle of its tail, while the mamba is notorious for both its speed and its potent strike. Even harmless species like the corn snake and garter snake are superbly adapted hunters, using a forked tongue to detect chemical traces in the air.\n\nLizards are equally diverse. The chameleon changes colour not primarily for camouflage — as commonly believed — but to communicate mood and regulate temperature. The Komodo dragon, the world's largest lizard, uses powerful jaws and bacteria-laden saliva to bring down prey as large as water buffalo. The basilisk — nicknamed the 'Jesus lizard' — can run across the surface of water on its hind legs. Geckos climb vertical glass using millions of microscopic hair-like structures on their feet. Each species occupies a distinct ecological niche.\n\nThe shell of a turtle or tortoise is not merely a protective case — it is part of the animal's skeleton, fused to the spine and ribcage. Sea turtles swim thousands of kilometres between feeding and nesting grounds, while snapping turtles hunt from ambush in freshwater. The distinction between turtles (aquatic, with flippers or webbed feet), tortoises (terrestrial, with dome-shaped shells and stumpy legs), and terrapins (freshwater, with clawed feet) is a useful vocabulary point that reflects genuine biological differences.\n\nCrocodilians — crocodiles, alligators, caimans, and the fish-eating gharial — are the closest living relatives of dinosaurs. A crocodile's jaw can exert the strongest bite force of any living animal, yet the muscles that open the jaw are so weak that a human can hold it shut with bare hands. Their eyes, nostrils, and ears are positioned on top of the head, allowing them to float almost invisibly at the water's surface — a hunting strategy unchanged for 200 million years.",
